import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';

// SITE-02@v1: The example names the agent's repeated addition of coordination code.
// SITE-04@v1: The example begins with an agent that has no previous session context.
// SITE-05@v1: One example connects the definition, index, code citation, review, and CLI result.
const rule = `# PDD-02 — Validate at Use
Token: PDD-02
Version: v1

## Rule
When code uses an artifact, validate the important facts.
A check from creation or preview does not stay true.

## Benefit
One use-time check protects every path to the boundary.

## Problem this prevents
Agents add locks and cleanup jobs for each timing gap.

## What this changes
- The download operation checks current permission.
- Tests change permission between preview and download.

## Exceptions
An immutable fact cannot change. Name that fact before
you omit the use-time check.

## History
- v1 (2026-08-24): Adopted after repeated timing fixes.`;
const index = `## Principles

- **PDD-02@v1 — Validate at Use** — Check critical facts at use.
  → \`docs/principles/pdd-02-validate-at-use.md\`

Read the full principle before you change covered code.`;
const code = `async function downloadExport(userId, exportId) {
  // PDD-02@v1: This download checks current permission before use.
  // The preview check only gives early feedback.
  await requireDownloadPermission(userId, exportId);
  return readExport(exportId);
}`;
const review = `Working as designed under PDD-02@v1.
The download checks current permission before it reads the export.
An old preview does not grant permission.`;
const output = `$ pdd check

src/exports/download.js:2 [PDD106]
PDD-02@v1 is stale; review this site against PDD-02@v2

pdd check: FAILED (1 problem)`;
const toc = [
  { id: 'the-problem', text: 'The problem' },
  { id: 'write-the-rule', text: '1. Write the rule' },
  { id: 'index-the-rule', text: '2. Index the rule' },
  { id: 'cite-the-dependency', text: '3. Cite the dependency' },
  { id: 'review-the-decision', text: '4. Review the decision' },
  { id: 'change-the-version', text: '5. Change the version' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="A complete example" description="Follow one PDD principle through its file, agent index, code comment, review, and version change." section="A complete example" toc={toc} previous={{ title: 'Introduction', href: '/' }} next={{ title: 'Set up your repository', href: '/blueprint' }}>
  <h1>A complete example</h1>
  <p class="doc-lead">One rule gives each new agent a clear decision and makes later changes visible.</p>
  <p>This example uses <a href="/principles/validate-at-use">Validate at Use</a>. It connects a permission rule to the code and reviews that depend on it.</p>

  <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
  <p>A new agent has no earlier context. Its task is to let a user download an export after a preview.</p>
  <p>The user can lose permission between the preview and the download. Agents repeatedly add locks, expiry jobs, and coordination code to close each gap.</p>
  <p>The team instead makes the download operation responsible for current permission. One check at that boundary replaces many earlier attempts to preserve permission.</p>

  <h2 id="write-the-rule">1. Write the rule</h2>
  <p>The principle file owns the full judgment, including its benefit and exceptions.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={rule} lang="markdown" filename="docs/principles/pdd-02-validate-at-use.md" />

  <h2 id="index-the-rule">2. Index the rule</h2>
  <p>The agent index gives the rule a short, discoverable entry.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={index} lang="markdown" filename="AGENTS.md" />
  <p><code>PDD-02</code> identifies the principle. <code>@v1</code> pins the entry to version one. The linked file supplies the full rule.</p>

  <h2 id="cite-the-dependency">3. Cite the dependency</h2>
  <p>The comment states the local dependency. It gives the next contributor a path to the complete rule.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={code} lang="javascript" filename="src/exports/download.js" />
  <p>The preview check gives early feedback. The download operation makes the permission decision from current facts.</p>

  <h2 id="review-the-decision">4. Review the decision</h2>
  <p>A reviewer cites the same token to explain why the earlier preview can become stale.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={review} filename="Review comment" />
  <p>Tests still cover the behavior. For example, a test removes permission after the preview and expects the download to fail.</p>

  <h2 id="change-the-version">5. Change the version</h2>
  <p>After a meaning change, the team advances the principle to <code>v2</code>. The agent index now cites <code>PDD-02@v2</code>. The old code citation still states <code>PDD-02@v1</code>.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={output} filename="Terminal" />
  <p>The CLI makes the old dependency visible. A contributor reads version two and reviews the download before changing its citation.</p>
  <div class="callout accent"><p><strong>A version change creates a review list.</strong>The CLI checks the citation. Reviewers and tests establish whether the code obeys the new rule.</p></div>
</BaseLayout>
